  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure remains an influence today even as it moves off to\nthe east today. Dry and mild to warm conditions will persist\ntoday with high temperatures reaching the 60s to around 70\ndegrees in the lowlands. The mountains will stay in the 50s to\nthe lower 60s. East-southeast winds will increase this afternoon\nas the high drifts off to the east. Gusts up to 15 or 20 mph \ncould be possible today as a result, mostly across the higher\nridges of the mountains.\n\nDid lower dew points for today as winds mix drier air down this\nafternoon. The dew points mixed with warmer temperatures will\ncreate RH percentage in the 20s and 30s this afternoon with the\nlowest across the mountains and typical warm spots in the\nlowlands. These low min RHs paired with breezy conditions could\ncreate fire weather concerns this afternoon across the\nmountains. The need for an SPS for fire weather danger will be\nevaluated this morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA low pressure center glides up the coast of the eastern CONUS \non Sunday. Rain chances will increase during the day Sunday as a\nresult across our eastern periphery mountain counties, especially\nfor Pocahontas County and the Greenbrier Valley. Light rainfall \namounts of up to a quarter of an inch could be picked up from\nthis system.\n\nA quick-moving, mostly dry cold front then approaches Sunday\nnight and crosses Monday morning. While mostly a dry front, a\nswath of light showers/drizzle could accompany its west to east\npassage Monday morning leading to a light spritzing of rain\nacross the area Monday. Drier weather and cooler temperatures\nmove in behind this front Monday night and Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nStill lacking cohesion in the models in regards to middle of\nthe week/Thanksgiving weather. One thing models do agree on is a\ndeep digging trough and stout low pressure center that will dig\nacross the Deep South and then rock up over our area as a\nnegative-tilt trough, signifying the potential for active \nweather and heavy precipitation. A second, flanking low pressure\ncenter is shown over the Great Lakes in both the EURO and GFS\nmodels indicating split flow troughing. \n\nThis feature and divergence in models with timing create some\nuncertainty in regards to weather type, amounts, impacts, and\noverall timing with this next weather maker as we move into\nWednesday and especially Thanksgiving Day. Rainfall across the\narea is probable, but the possibility for some snow in the\nhigher elevations of mountains may be possible as well with\ncolder temperatures in place.",
